linked_issues.body:
### Description

UBJSON and BJData high-precision (`H`) numbers have a length-delimited text payload. With the default NUL handling, the reader accepts a valid numeric prefix followed by a NUL and arbitrary bytes inside that payload.

For example, `48 69 03 31 00 78` (`H`, `i`, length 3, `1`, NUL, `x`) is accepted as the unsigned integer `1` by both `from_ubjson` and `from_bjdata`.

### Expected vs. actual results

Expected: reject the malformed high-precision number, as for other invalid number text (parse_error.115). The [UBJSON specification](https://ubjson.org/type-reference/value-types/#high-precision-numbers-larger-than-64-bit) requires high-precision values to follow JSON's number syntax. A NUL is not a number character or whitespace, and the payload length is explicit.

Actual: the JSON lexer reports end_of_input at the NUL; `get_ubjson_high_precision_number()` checks that token but does not distinguish a NUL from the real end of the bounded payload. Bytes following the NUL are consumed by the binary reader and silently ignored by the number lexer. This also makes these binary inputs behave differently with JSON_STRICT_NUL_HANDLING enabled, despite that macro being documented as affecting only JSON text.

### Minimal code example

```cpp
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    const std::vector<std::uint8_t> input = {'H', 'i', 3, '1', 0, 'x'};
    std::cout << nlohmann::json::from_ubjson(input) << '\n';
    std::cout << nlohmann::json::from_bjdata(input) << '\n';
}
```

Both calls print `1`.

### Compiler and operating system

MinGW GCC 15.1.0, Windows x86_64, `g++ -std=c++11 -I include repro.cpp`.

### Library version

Unmodified `develop` at `6d0867a85f0148ae184f457ec6755f9d4e184132`. The same token-only check is present on `master` at `55f93686c01528224f448c19128836e7df245f72` (source inspection; not run there).

I intend to submit a small check in the shared high-precision reader, with regression coverage for both formats, NUL at the last payload byte, bytes hidden after NUL, nested inputs, valid numbers, and unchanged JSON text behavior. Searches for the symptom and inspection of related PRs did not reveal an existing fix for this boundary. Investigation and patch preparation use AI assistance.

